WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:03.860
Which activities are the most
effective at different levels

00:00:03.930 --> 00:00:05.830
of competence in a language?

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:08.320
So the relationship between
learning activities

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:11.129
and where we are in the
language. I'm often asked, "What

00:00:11.129 --> 00:00:12.480
do you do to learn a language?"

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:14.010
"What do I do when I'm starting out?"

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.490
"What do I do if I want to
break through the plateau?"

00:00:16.509 --> 00:00:18.400
We are all at different
levels in the language.

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:22.290
And therefore, to some extent,
our learning activities will vary.

00:00:22.309 --> 00:00:23.680
What is my situation?

00:00:23.689 --> 00:00:25.350
Because I can only talk about what I do.

00:00:25.389 --> 00:00:30.240
So first of all, when it comes to the sort
of levels in a language, there are some

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:34.770
recognized reference points, like
the Common European Framework of

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:39.620
Reference, which has six levels of
skill, you know, A1, A2, which is a

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:44.410
beginner, B1, B2, which is intermediate,
and C1, C2, which is advanced.

00:00:44.439 --> 00:00:47.980
And these can be for writing, for
speaking, for reading, for listening.

00:00:48.045 --> 00:00:51.445
To some extent, it gives you an idea
of what that level corresponds to.

00:00:51.445 --> 00:00:53.175
But I have a lot of trouble with that.

00:00:53.175 --> 00:00:57.855
I prefer to see our levels in a
language are sort of on a continuum.

00:00:57.925 --> 00:01:01.435
When we start, we're very much
influenced by our own language.

00:01:01.435 --> 00:01:05.375
We're sort of translating from our own
language structures that we think in,

00:01:05.375 --> 00:01:09.895
in terms of our own language,
and gradually over time, we sort of

00:01:10.359 --> 00:01:12.809
get more comfortable
in this other language.

00:01:12.839 --> 00:01:16.570
Some people talk about "interlanguages,"
sort of a language that we use when

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:21.199
we're sort of in between the new language
and languages that we already know.

00:01:21.309 --> 00:01:22.880
I don't think that's particularly helpful.

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:27.839
I think we just gradually get better and
there's sort of infinite steps on this

00:01:27.869 --> 00:01:32.059
gradient. And also we might be better in
listening or in reading or in speaking.

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:37.989
And so to arbitrarily say, you know, "I'm a
B2" or "I'm a B1," I'm reluctant to do that.

00:01:38.175 --> 00:01:40.714
And while I say that,
it is a handy reference.

00:01:40.725 --> 00:01:45.544
So I always think in terms of getting to
sort of a B2 level, which to me is sort

00:01:45.544 --> 00:01:48.815
of fluent to the extent that I can use
the language comfortably. Knowing that

00:01:48.815 --> 00:01:51.475
I'm going to make mistakes. Knowing that
there are going to be some things that I

00:01:51.475 --> 00:01:53.645
don't understand. But I'm communicating.

00:01:54.664 --> 00:01:59.375
To me, that's sort of you're launched
now. Whereas beginner to me, either

00:01:59.375 --> 00:02:02.504
you are a total beginner, like zero,
and I'll tell you what I do when I'm

00:02:02.505 --> 00:02:06.895
at zero, or you're still very much
struggling to get a toehold in the

00:02:06.895 --> 00:02:08.675
language, that's the sort of beginner.

00:02:08.735 --> 00:02:11.765
Then you've got B2, which to me
is sort of the launching point.

00:02:11.865 --> 00:02:16.635
And then somewhere in between those two
is the sort of B1, genuine intermediate

00:02:16.835 --> 00:02:20.645
level. In Canada, for whatever
reason, they came up with 12 levels.

00:02:20.645 --> 00:02:25.175
They call it the "Canadian Language
Benchmarks," on the theory that you can

00:02:25.235 --> 00:02:28.565
tie a particular job to level five.

00:02:28.605 --> 00:02:31.764
And they try to describe in
great detail what you are able

00:02:31.765 --> 00:02:33.204
to do in a particular level.

00:02:33.224 --> 00:02:35.345
And I think this is
also quite unrealistic.

00:02:35.394 --> 00:02:38.054
You know, sometimes we do
better than at other times.

00:02:38.475 --> 00:02:42.575
Depending on what we've been exposed
to, we might do better in certain

00:02:42.615 --> 00:02:44.755
types of contexts than in others.

00:02:44.815 --> 00:02:46.305
It's more or less the same idea.

00:02:46.305 --> 00:02:49.614
We go from nothing, from
zero, to being launched.

00:02:49.655 --> 00:02:54.915
And if we are a non-native speaker, there
will still be some jagged edges in our

00:02:54.915 --> 00:02:56.724
language skill, even after we're launched.

00:02:56.725 --> 00:02:59.714
And we just continue to
progress as long as we keep studying.

00:03:00.005 --> 00:03:03.245
So when I'm a complete beginner, and
I might just demonstrate this sometime

00:03:03.245 --> 00:03:07.255
at LingQ, because that's what I use
now, but before LingQ, what I would do

00:03:07.255 --> 00:03:11.195
is, I would go out and buy two or three
different starter books, Teach Yourself,

00:03:11.265 --> 00:03:16.045
Living Language, Colloquial, Assimil,
they're all the same in my opinion.

00:03:16.055 --> 00:03:16.525
They have

00:03:16.799 --> 00:03:20.510
a little lesson, they have a vocabulary
list for the lesson, and they

00:03:20.510 --> 00:03:24.209
have some grammatical explanations
that you kind of look at. And you

00:03:24.209 --> 00:03:25.590
work your way through the lesson.

00:03:25.790 --> 00:03:29.420
And if you listen to that, and then the
next one and the next one, and then you

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:32.689
go back to the first one and you keep
doing that, eventually you start to

00:03:32.690 --> 00:03:34.560
get a bit of a sense of the language.

00:03:34.630 --> 00:03:37.810
Of course, by doing three different
books, I'm getting the same material

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:39.079
from three different sources.

00:03:39.109 --> 00:03:41.489
It's what I often refer to as "grazing."

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:44.049
You're kind of covering the
same material in different ways.

00:03:44.239 --> 00:03:45.730
That's a very effective way to learn.

00:03:46.035 --> 00:03:50.415
Because if we are constantly using
the same book and trying to force

00:03:50.424 --> 00:03:53.674
the same material into our brains,
the brain starts to lose interest.

00:03:53.725 --> 00:03:58.895
As Spitzer always says, the brain likes
repetition, but the brain likes novelty.

00:03:58.954 --> 00:04:01.194
So you keep it fresh
by changing the source.

00:04:02.005 --> 00:04:06.554
You don't want to cover too broad a
range of vocabulary at the beginning.

00:04:06.614 --> 00:04:08.114
You want it to be more or less...

00:04:08.359 --> 00:04:11.840
It's high frequency vocabulary so
that there will be enough repetition

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:13.549
that you can kind of get somewhere.

00:04:13.579 --> 00:04:17.880
I think any learning content we have,
you want to have enough of a mix of the

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:23.200
familiar with the new so that we're not
like everything is brand new. And

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:26.670
we have the feeling that we're trying
to learn things and no sooner have we

00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:28.260
grabbed it, then it's gone, you know?

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:31.560
And that's how we designed the
Mini Stories. That there's in

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:35.469
each lesson, the same verbs, the
same words repeat five times.

00:04:35.525 --> 00:04:39.965
We need this level of repetition at the
beginning. And the traditional starter

00:04:39.965 --> 00:04:43.435
books that I used to use don't have
enough repetition. Because they take you

00:04:43.445 --> 00:04:47.544
from the train station to the doctor's
office, to the hotel lobby or customs or

00:04:47.544 --> 00:04:49.734
whatever they usually do in those books.

00:04:49.744 --> 00:04:55.704
So as a beginner, I like to be in content
with high-frequency words, ideally with a

00:04:55.704 --> 00:04:57.835
lot of repetition. And I end up listening

00:04:58.039 --> 00:05:02.400
over and over and over again until I
start to get a toehold in the language.

00:05:02.409 --> 00:05:03.539
That is the pattern.

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:07.260
And even after having completed one
of these beginner books, where they

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:12.069
promise you that you'll be at B1 or
B2, that's not the case. Because we

00:05:12.149 --> 00:05:14.330
require so much input in the language.

00:05:14.359 --> 00:05:17.949
And to the extent that we have
high-frequency vocabulary, that

00:05:17.949 --> 00:05:19.729
means we're left with a lot of

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:23.310
lower-frequency vocabulary
that we still don't know.

00:05:23.450 --> 00:05:26.870
And therefore we're very limited
in what we can listen to and read.

00:05:26.990 --> 00:05:31.920
So it doesn't take you to B2 or
B1, but it takes you maybe to an A2

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:35.230
level in the language, if we stay
with the common European framework.

00:05:35.260 --> 00:05:39.200
Now, if I'm going to study a language
that I'm at an intermediate level in,

00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:41.160
there are two possible situations.

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:44.380
Either I'm in the midst of learning
that language, or it's a language

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:47.810
that I learned before and I left it
for a while and I'm going back to it.

00:05:48.190 --> 00:05:49.580
The approach is essentially the same.

00:05:50.405 --> 00:05:56.355
I want to go to content that is a little
bit challenging, but interesting to me.

00:05:56.844 --> 00:05:59.965
Some of that, which I then vary
with something like the Mini

00:05:59.965 --> 00:06:01.515
Stories with a lot of repetition.

00:06:01.924 --> 00:06:07.124
I am particularly fond of Sentence Review
now at LingQ because now that I know more

00:06:07.134 --> 00:06:11.625
about how the brain learns languages.
When we study sentence by sentence, we are

00:06:11.625 --> 00:06:14.515
focusing more on that particular sentence.

00:06:14.885 --> 00:06:18.395
And we can hear the sentence again,
and we're looking at words within

00:06:18.395 --> 00:06:20.284
that sentence. And then we review

00:06:20.489 --> 00:06:22.760
the words immediately after that sentence.

00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:26.880
So there's a certain level of
frequency, a certain level of familiar

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:30.570
things, and all of that helps to
get those neurons firing together.

00:06:30.659 --> 00:06:34.520
And then in the Sentence Review,
we were forced to reassemble the

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:38.520
sentence, which means drawing on our...
it's not exactly our memory because

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:39.890
we're looking at the words there.

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:42.630
But we're having to form a sentence.

00:06:42.659 --> 00:06:44.140
The words have been scrambled.

00:06:44.140 --> 00:06:47.450
We have to put them back together
again in their proper order.

00:06:47.659 --> 00:06:51.649
It's a very good discipline for
becoming familiar with the structure

00:06:51.650 --> 00:06:55.589
of the language and also sort of
renewing our acquaintance with

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:57.530
vocabulary that we have just met.

00:06:57.985 --> 00:07:02.485
So I find that a tremendous exercise,
even in the initial stages, but

00:07:02.495 --> 00:07:06.465
particularly as we progress to kind
of get that feel that you're getting a

00:07:06.465 --> 00:07:08.055
good sense of how the language works.

00:07:08.115 --> 00:07:10.785
I don't spend all my time doing
that because I spend a lot of time

00:07:10.785 --> 00:07:13.645
just plain listening and reading,
you know. More of a top-down thing.

00:07:13.914 --> 00:07:18.424
But I find that that "nuts and bolts, bits
and pieces" exercise is very effective.

00:07:18.465 --> 00:07:20.044
That's kind of at the intermediate level.

00:07:20.145 --> 00:07:23.375
But once you get past that, now
you're into audio book territory.

00:07:23.394 --> 00:07:24.924
You're into reading books.

00:07:24.945 --> 00:07:27.424
You're into extensive
exposure to the language.

00:07:27.965 --> 00:07:30.355
And that's the only way you're
going to take yourself from that

00:07:30.434 --> 00:07:34.405
"I have studied this language and I can
do a limited number of things in the

00:07:34.405 --> 00:07:36.865
language" to being really comfortable.

00:07:37.095 --> 00:07:42.775
And being a smooth speaker, writer, and
to be able to understand movies and so

00:07:42.775 --> 00:07:44.635
forth. You have to consume a lot of it.

00:07:44.935 --> 00:07:46.795
And that's the extensive stage.

00:07:46.945 --> 00:07:48.075
That's the reward.

00:07:48.155 --> 00:07:50.145
Every stage in the process is a reward.

00:07:50.165 --> 00:07:54.754
I love doing my Sentence Review, even as
a beginner or as an intermediate learner.

00:07:55.114 --> 00:07:58.385
But then eventually when I'm able to
enjoy books and movies and so forth,

00:07:58.474 --> 00:08:00.844
that's where the big payback comes.

